@database "sub_agent_manager"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: sub_agent_manager.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Sub-Agent Manager"
@{h1}Sub-Agent Manager

An agent with a bigger job hands parts of it to other agents: a researcher
for a question, a reviewer for a change, three workers side by side. The
Sub-Agent Manager (SAM) lets it do that. Each sub-agent runs in a session
of its own, keeps its history, and can be given follow-up work later
instead of starting over. The coordinating agent sees what its sub-agents
are doing -- running, idle, or how their last run ended -- and you see the
same in the @{b}Sub-Agents@{ub} panel, down to the sub-agents of
sub-agents, with every transcript.

A SAM is a server entry; there can be many, each with its own list of
agents it may start and its own limits. Which agents a SAM may start is
decided there, not by the model.

@{image docs/panel.png "The Sub-Agents panel: the sub-agents of a session with what each is doing"}

@{code}
For users
  @{"  The Sub-Agents panel  " link panel}

For agents and developers
  @{"  The manage_sub_agent tool  " link tool}
  @{"  Instances, limits and waking  " link rules}
  @{"  The sub-agent list hook  " link hook}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Web endpoints  " link endpoints}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
  @{"  Making an agent spawnable  " link register}
@{body}
@endnode

@node panel "The Sub-Agents panel"
@{h1}The Sub-Agents panel

Open it from the launcher (@{b}Agents & tools@{ub}, "Sub-Agents"), or from
a session's info button, which opens it on that session. Every SAM
instance has a panel of its own, and each shows the same thing: the
sub-agents of the session, whichever instance started them.

@{h2}Which session it shows

The panel follows the chat: it shows the sub-agents of the session open
there and switches when you switch sessions. Opened from a session's info
button it is pinned to that session instead; the toolbar says so and lets
go of it again. With no session open it says so and asks for nothing. It
reloads every 10 seconds; the menu next to the reload button sets another
interval or turns it off. Only your own sessions are shown: a session of
another user reads as one without sub-agents. An admin -- and everyone
while authentication is off -- sees every user's sessions, as in the other
plugin panels.

@{h2}The map

The panel opens on the @{b}Map@{ub}: the session at the top and every
sub-agent below it, nested -- a sub-agent that started sub-agents of its
own carries them under it. Each node shows the task (or the agent type),
the agent type, the instance id, the messages of its transcript, the
tokens of its last model call, how long it was at it (from its creation to
its last use) and its state. A running node also shows what it is doing
right now. Click a node to open its transcript. On a very large tree the
map stops at 300 nodes, 60 session reads or 20 levels and says that not
everything is shown.

@{image docs/map.png "The map: a session, its sub-agents and a sub-agent's own sub-agent"}

@{h2}The list

The @{b}List@{ub} shows the level right below the session, with figures at
the top: all sub-agents, and how many are running, idle, interrupted, and
failed, cancelled or archived. If the SAM filters agents by workflow phase,
a line names the phase and the agents it lets the SAM start.

The filter in the toolbar (on the List only) shows the open ones
(running, idle, interrupted), only the running ones, or all, archived
included. Each card has the state, the instance id, the agent type, the
messages and tokens, when it was last used, the task, and for a running
one its current activity.

@{table}
State | Means
Running | a run is under way, in this process or in any other
Idle | its last run is done; the answer is in the transcript
Interrupted | its run was cut off -- the process running it ended first
Failed | its last run ended with an error
Cancelled | its last run was stopped
Archived | put away by its coordinator or by you; its history stays
@{body}

The tokens are the prompt tokens of the sub-agent's last model call as the
provider counted them, with the share of the model's window, or "(compacted
since)" once its history was compacted. They come from the context usage
tracker; without that plugin the figure is left out.

@{h2}What you can do

@{table}
Button | Shown on | Does
Transcript | every card, every node of the map | opens the transcript in a drawer
Archive | an idle, running or interrupted one | archives it, after asking
Earlier messages | the drawer, while there are older ones | loads older messages above the ones shown
@{body}

The drawer shows the session, agent type, state, times and task, then the
most recent messages (20 by default), each with its role, its text, and
the tool calls it made. Archiving keeps the sub-agent's history, and its
coordinator can still continue it; a running one runs to its end.

The panel only reads: it never marks anything interrupted, unlike the
tool's @{tt}list@{utt}. When the server refuses -- the sub-agent is gone,
or is not in this session -- the panel shows a notice; after an archive
it also loads the list anew. When the list cannot be loaded it says so and shows none of the old
one; the map is loaded on its own and fails on its own.
@endnode

@node tool "The manage_sub_agent tool"
@{h1}The manage_sub_agent tool

Each SAM instance gives an agent one tool, named after the instance:
@{tt}<instance>_manage_sub_agent@{utt} (@{tt}sub_agent_manager_manage_sub_agent@{utt}
in the default setup). Every call names an @{tt}operation@{utt}.

@{h2}Operations

@{table}
operation | Needs | Does
create | agent_type, task | starts a new sub-agent with the task; waits for its answer unless blocking is false
continue | instance_id, message | sends a follow-up; the sub-agent sees its whole history; always waits
poll | instance_id | how a background run stands, at once
wait | instance_id | waits until it ends, at most default_wait_timeout
wait_all | instance_ids | waits for several at once
cancel | instance_id | stops a running one
list | -- | this instance's sub-agents of the session and their state
info | instance_id | a page of the transcript
delete | instance_id or instance_ids | archives
@{body}

When @{tt}operation@{utt} is missing, two argument shapes are taken as
meant: @{tt}agent_type@{utt} without @{tt}instance_id@{utt} is a create,
@{tt}instance_id@{utt} with a @{tt}message@{utt} (or @{tt}task@{utt}) and no
@{tt}agent_type@{utt} is a continue. Anything else answers "Missing
'operation' parameter".

@{h2}Parameters

@{table}
Parameter | Used by | Meaning
agent_type | create | the name of the agent's server entry; must be allowed by this SAM (see @{" Making an agent spawnable " link register})
task | create | the first message; continue takes it when message is missing
instance_label | create | goes into the instance id: sub_<label>_<number>, characters other than letters, digits, _ and - become _; without it sub_<agent_type>_<number>
blocking | create | true (default): the answer comes when the run is over; false: the run goes on in the background, answer status running
wake_when_done | create | with blocking false: the session is woken when the run ends (see @{" Instances, limits and waking " link rules}); default false
use_advanced_model | create, continue | runs the agent on its advanced model chain; default false; ignored when the SAM sets allow_advanced_model false, and on continue for the agent types in advanced_create_only_agents
instance_id | continue, poll, wait, cancel, info, delete | the id an answer returned
instance_ids | wait_all, delete | a list; duplicates count once
message | continue | the follow-up
include_completed | list | true: archived ones too; failed and cancelled ones are always listed
limit | info | messages per page; default 20, at most 200 (info_default_limit, info_max_limit)
offset | info | 0-based index to start at; without it the page is the last limit messages
max_chars | info | each text and each tool call's arguments are cut after it, saying how much was left out; default 4000; 0 or less: no cut
@{body}

@{h2}Answers

@{bullet}@{b}create@{ub} (waiting) and @{b}continue@{ub}: @{tt}instance_id@{utt},
@{tt}status: completed@{utt} (the run is over), @{tt}outcome@{utt}
(@{tt}completed@{utt}, or @{tt}error@{utt}/@{tt}cancelled@{utt} when the run
aborted -- @{tt}result@{utt} then starts with "Error:" or "Cancelled:"),
@{tt}result@{utt} (the sub-agent's answer), @{tt}message_count@{utt} and
@{tt}agent_type@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{b}create@{ub} with @{tt}blocking: false@{utt}: @{tt}instance_id@{utt},
@{tt}status: running@{utt}, @{tt}agent_type@{utt} and a @{tt}message@{utt}
that says whether this session will be woken.
@{bullet}@{b}list@{ub}: @{tt}instances@{utt} -- each with @{tt}instance_id@{utt},
@{tt}agent_type@{utt}, @{tt}status@{utt} (@{tt}running@{utt}, @{tt}idle@{utt},
@{tt}interrupted@{utt}, @{tt}failed@{utt}, @{tt}cancelled@{utt},
@{tt}archived@{utt}), @{tt}created_at@{utt}, @{tt}last_used@{utt},
@{tt}task_summary@{utt} (the first 100 characters of the task),
@{tt}current_activity@{utt}, @{tt}message_count@{utt} -- and
@{tt}count@{utt}. A sub-agent found with an activity but no run behind it is
written @{tt}interrupted@{utt} here.
@{bullet}@{b}info@{ub}: the instance's @{tt}status@{utt}, times, task and
@{tt}message_count@{utt}, @{tt}messages@{utt} (each with @{tt}index@{utt},
@{tt}role@{utt}, @{tt}content@{utt}, @{tt}tool_calls@{utt} with name and
arguments, @{tt}tool_name@{utt} on a tool result) and @{tt}window@{utt}
(@{tt}mode@{utt} tail or offset, @{tt}start_index@{utt},
@{tt}returned@{utt}, @{tt}total@{utt}, @{tt}has_more_before@{utt},
@{tt}has_more_after@{utt}).
@{bullet}@{b}poll@{ub} and @{b}wait@{ub}: @{tt}status@{utt} @{tt}running@{utt},
@{tt}completed@{utt} with @{tt}result@{utt}, @{tt}failed@{utt} with
@{tt}error@{utt}, @{tt}cancelled@{utt} (with @{tt}error@{utt} only when the
run answered "Cancelled: ..."), or @{tt}interrupted@{utt}. The
first poll or wait that sees the ending takes the result with it; later
ones read it from the stored transcript (its last answer).
@{bullet}@{b}wait_all@{ub}: @{tt}results@{utt} (one answer as wait gives it
per id), @{tt}total@{utt}, @{tt}completed@{utt}, @{tt}failed@{utt} (failed,
cancelled, interrupted and errors together).
@{bullet}@{b}cancel@{ub}: @{tt}cancelled@{utt} for a background run;
@{tt}cancelling@{utt} for a waiting create or continue, which stops at its
next step.
@{bullet}@{b}delete@{ub}: @{tt}status: archived@{utt} for one id; for
several @{tt}archived_count@{utt}, @{tt}failed_count@{utt}, @{tt}total@{utt}
and @{tt}results@{utt}.

A refused call answers @{tt}status: error@{utt} with @{tt}error@{utt}, and
for some an @{tt}error_type@{utt}: @{tt}missing_parameter@{utt},
@{tt}agent_blocked@{utt}, @{tt}phase_blocked@{utt},
@{tt}agent_role_gate@{utt} (the caller's role may not run that agent),
@{tt}session_locked@{utt} or @{tt}foreign_session@{utt} (the agent refused
the run before it started). A limit answers @{tt}status: limit_reached@{utt}.

@{h2}An example

@{code json}
{"operation": "create", "agent_type": "research_agent", "task": "Compare the SLAs of the three vendors", "blocking": false}
{"operation": "create", "agent_type": "research_agent", "task": "Summarise their pricing pages", "blocking": false}
{"operation": "wait_all", "instance_ids": ["sub_research_agent_4803112", "sub_research_agent_4803113"]}
{"operation": "continue", "instance_id": "sub_research_agent_4803112", "message": "Which of them covers restores?"}
{"operation": "info", "instance_id": "sub_research_agent_4803112", "offset": 0, "limit": 20}
@{body}

Instance ids are made by the SAM; an agent uses the id an answer returned,
never one it puts together from a label.
@endnode

@node rules "Instances, limits and waking"
@{h1}Instances, limits and waking

@{h2}A sub-agent is a session

@{tt}create@{utt} makes a session for the sub-agent below the caller's
session and runs the agent in it; @{tt}continue@{utt} runs it again in the
same session. The sub-agent inherits the caller's session variables when it
is created. On every continue they are refreshed from the caller's current
ones, except those the sub-agent set itself. A caller whose own session was
not saved yet gets one ("Coordinator Session").

One instance never runs twice at the same time: a continue while it runs
is refused ("already running", or "already running in another process"),
so wait for it first. @{tt}cancel@{utt} reaches the run's own tool calls
and sub-agents too.

@{h2}Which sub-agents an instance sees

@{tt}list@{utt}, @{tt}poll@{utt} and the hook see only the sub-agents this
SAM instance started. @{tt}continue@{utt}, @{tt}info@{utt} and
@{tt}delete@{utt} take any sub-agent of the calling session. The panel
shows all of them.

@{h2}Limits

@{bullet}@{tt}max_sub_agents_per_type@{utt} and
@{tt}max_sub_agents_per_session@{utt} count the sub-agents stored as open:
running, idle, and an interrupted one until the tool's @{tt}list@{utt}
marks it interrupted (the panel and the hook show it interrupted before
that). Failed, cancelled, archived and marked ones do not count. The type's limit is checked first.
@{bullet}At a limit a create answers @{tt}limit_reached@{utt}. With
@{tt}auto_archive_on_limit: true@{utt} the oldest counted one of that type
(or of the session) is archived instead, even while it runs, and the create
goes on.
@{bullet}Continuing a sub-agent that is not counted -- interrupted, failed,
cancelled, archived -- takes a place like a new one: the limits apply, and
an archived one is open again.
@{bullet}@{tt}max_nesting_depth@{utt} is how many levels of sub-agents this
SAM allows below its caller. A sub-agent inherits the rest; a SAM further
down can only lower it. With none left, create answers "Maximum nesting
depth exceeded".

@{h2}Background runs and waking

@{tt}create@{utt} with @{tt}blocking: false@{utt} starts the run and answers
at once. The caller then polls or waits, or -- with
@{tt}wake_when_done: true@{utt} -- ends its turn and is woken when the run
ends, however it ends, except by its own cancel or delete. The answer of
the create says whether the wake is armed. It is not when session presence
is switched off, when the call runs on a session nobody continues, when
the caller is itself a sub-agent (its caller takes its answer), or when the
chain of wakes is already as deep as it may go; the answer names the
reason, and the caller should stay awake and poll.

A background run lives in the process that started it: the API server, or
an @{tt}agent-cli chat@{utt}. A one-shot @{tt}agent-cli run@{utt} that
ends its turn takes its background runs with it. A run whose process ended
reads as interrupted afterwards.

An ending nobody picks up is kept in memory for an hour once it is stored,
for a day when it could not be stored; after that a poll reads the stored
state and the transcript.
@endnode

@node hook "The sub-agent list hook"
@{h1}The sub-agent list hook

@{tt}inject_sub_agent_context@{utt} runs before each model call and shows
the agent its sub-agents, so it continues or waits on them instead of
starting a second one for the same job. It is off until an agent switches
it on.

@{h2}What the model gets

A message at the end of the history, from the plugin: the agents this SAM
may start, a table of this instance's sub-agents of the session -- open
ones first, then the newest -- and what the states mean. Archived ones are
left out unless @{tt}show_completed@{utt} is true.

@{code markdown}
## Sub-Agents

**Available agents:** research_agent, code_reviewer

| Type | Instance ID | Status | Task |
|------|-------------|--------|------|
| research_agent | `sub_research_agent_4803113` | running | Summarise their pricing pages |
| research_agent | `sub_research_agent_4803112` | idle | Compare the SLAs of the three vendors |

running: working now. idle: its last run is done; poll or info for the answer, continue for more. interrupted, failed, cancelled: its last run did not finish.
Continue: `sub_agent_manager_manage_sub_agent(operation='continue', instance_id='...', message='...')`
@{body}

The "Available agents" line names the phase's agents while phase filtering
is on and a phase is set -- by the session, or else by the agent's
configured default; otherwise @{tt}allowed_agents@{utt} as written, and
nothing when that contains @{tt}*@{utt}. With @{tt}format: text@{utt} the
block is a plain numbered list. When the last sub-agent is archived, the
hook says so once ("None left -- every sub-agent of this session is
archived.").

The message is added only when it differs from the last one the hook
added. It carries no counts or times, so it stays the same until a
sub-agent comes, goes or changes state; the earlier ones stay where they
are and the cached prompt in front of them is untouched.

@{h2}When it does nothing

@{bullet}its @{tt}enabled@{utt} setting is false;
@{bullet}the call has no session or no messages;
@{bullet}the session's sub-agents cannot be read (a new session not saved yet);
@{bullet}there are none and there never were in this history;
@{bullet}the block would be the same as the last one.

An error is logged and the model call goes on without a block. The hook
has 5 seconds.

@{h2}Switching it on

In the agent's YAML, with the SAM instance's name in front:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  hooks:
    enabled: true
    overrides:
      research_sam.inject_sub_agent_context:
        enabled: true
        max_sub_agents_shown: 4
@{body}

@{h2}Settings

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
enabled | true | false: the hook stays silent even where an agent switched it on
max_sub_agents_shown | 10 | how many rows: open ones first, then the newest
show_completed | false | true: archived ones too
format | markdown | markdown, or text
@{body}

The defaults come from the code; the SAM's server entry changes them for
every agent in a @{tt}hook_config.inject_sub_agent_context@{utt} block,
and the agent's own override wins over both.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tool

The tool description and every parameter's description come from the
plugin's @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}, about 4,000 characters in every request of
an agent that has the tool. Its "Available:" list is drawn anew each time
the tools are listed: the registered agents that are visible to tools or
the UI and that this SAM allows. With phase filtering on, a note says the
list depends on the phase.

@{h2}Errors it gets

The texts say what to change, for example:

@{bullet}"Missing required parameter(s) for 'create': task. 'create' needs 'agent_type' (one of: research_agent) and 'task' (the instruction text for the sub-agent)."
@{bullet}"Agent 'x' not allowed by this sub-agent manager. Allowed agents: research_agent"
@{bullet}"Agent 'x' not allowed in current phase 'planning'. Allowed agents for this phase: ..."
@{bullet}"Sub-agent 'x' not found. This session's sub-agents are: ... Use the instance_id exactly as returned, never assembled from a label and a guessed number." (the 30 most recently used)
@{bullet}"Maximum number of active sub-agents of type 'research_agent' (3) reached."
@{bullet}"Sub-agent 'x' is already running. Cannot execute the same sub-agent instance concurrently. Wait for current execution to complete."
@{bullet}"Timeout waiting for instance 'x' (waited 3600.2s)" -- the sub-agent runs on.

@{h2}The status line

The chat's status line ends with the result: "Created sub-agent x (type:
y), 1234 chars", "Continued sub-agent ...", "Async execution started: x",
"Listed 3 sub-agents: a, b, c", "Retrieved info for x (type: y, showing 20
of 45 messages)", "Completed: 2, Failed: 0 of 2 instances", "Cancelled x",
"Archived sub-agent x (type: y)". An aborted run shows as an error line.

@{h2}Slash commands

In the chat, @{tt}/subagents@{utt} runs @{tt}list@{utt} and
@{tt}/cancel <instance-id>@{utt} runs @{tt}cancel@{utt} -- the same call
the agent makes, with the same checks.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

Tool answers are ordinary results appended to the history. A waiting
create or continue brings back the sub-agent's whole answer; the
sub-agent's own work stays in its session, which is the point of it.
@{tt}info@{utt} is paged and cut per message, so one call cannot pour a
long transcript into the caller's context. The hook appends its block only
when it changed and never rewrites anything before it.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}@{tt}continue@{utt} does not check @{tt}allowed_agents@{utt} or
the phase again -- only the role gate. A sub-agent stays continuable after
its type was taken off the list.
@{bullet}An agent missing from the "Available" list because it is private
can still be started by its exact name when the SAM allows it.
@{bullet}How long @{tt}wait@{utt} waits is the server's
@{tt}default_wait_timeout@{utt}; a call cannot ask for another.
@{bullet}The limits are counted under a lock per process: two processes
starting sub-agents in the same session at the same moment can pass them.
@endnode

@node endpoints "Web endpoints"
@{h1}Web endpoints

Under @{tt}/plugins/<instance>/@{utt}, for the panel. Each takes the parent
session as @{tt}session_id@{utt} and answers only for the signed-in user's
own sessions ("anonymous" without a login); for an admin, and for everyone
while authentication is off, for every user's (@{tt}auth/session_access.py@{utt}).

@{table}
Method and path | Answers
GET / | the panel
GET /sub-agents | every sub-agent of the session, archived ones included, whichever instance started it: the stored fields, state, message_count, context_tokens, context_window, context_stale; and phase (null without phase filtering)
GET /agent-map | the session and everything below it, nested (children), and truncated when the map stopped at 300 nodes, 60 session reads or 20 levels
GET /sub-agents/{id} | the transcript, paged as info pages it (offset, limit)
DELETE /sub-agents/{id} | archives it, as delete does
@{body}

On the @{tt}/sub-agents/{id}@{utt} routes a sub-agent that is not in the
session, or a session that is not yours, answers 404; the list and the
map answer a session that is not yours as an empty one. Any other
failure answers 500 with its reason. The list and the map only read: unlike the tool's list they never mark anything interrupted.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The plugin needs @{tt}anyio@{utt}, which comes with the web server. The
default entry in @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    sub_agent_manager:
      type: sub_agent_manager
      enabled: true
      max_nesting_depth: 5
      max_sub_agents_per_type: 6
      auto_archive_on_limit: true
      allowed_agents:
        - research_agent
        - sysadmin_agent
        - comfyui_agent
        - chat_agent
      hook_config:
        enabled: true
        inject_sub_agent_context:
          enabled: true
          max_sub_agents_shown: 5
          format: "markdown"
@{body}

An agent gets the tool when its allowlist names the instance:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "+sub_agent_manager/*"
@{body}

A harness usually brings its own instance next to its agents, with its
own list and limits: @{tt}skills_sam@{utt}, @{tt}coder_sam@{utt},
@{tt}gamedev_sam@{utt}, @{tt}research_sam@{utt}, @{tt}debate_sam@{utt}
and @{tt}panel_sam@{utt} are such instances.

@{h2}Server settings

Every key is a top-level key of the server entry. The @{tt}config:@{utt}
block in the plugin's @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt} only repeats the defaults;
it is not read.

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
allowed_agents | ["*"] | which agents it may start: server entry names or patterns (fnmatch)
blocked_agents | [] | never these, whatever allowed_agents says; exact names
max_nesting_depth | 5 | levels of sub-agents below the caller
max_sub_agents_per_session | 10 | running or idle sub-agents per session
max_sub_agents_per_type | 3 | the same, per agent type
auto_archive_on_limit | false | true: archive the oldest instead of answering limit_reached
default_wait_timeout | 3600 | seconds wait and wait_all wait
allow_advanced_model | true | false: use_advanced_model is ignored (logged)
advanced_create_only_agents | [] | agent types whose use_advanced_model counts on create only
info_default_limit | 20 | messages per info page without limit
info_max_limit | 200 | the most one info page returns
info_default_max_chars | 4000 | the cut per message without max_chars
hook_config | none | the hook's settings, see @{" The sub-agent list hook " link hook}
phase_filtering | off | see below
@{body}

@{h2}Phase filtering

A SAM can narrow the agents it starts to a workflow phase, read from a
session variable:

@{code yaml}
      phase_filtering:
        enabled: true
        phase_variable: workflow_phase
        phase_agents:
          research: [research_agent]
          review: [code_reviewer]
          _default: []
@{body}

The phase is the session's variable, or else the default the calling
agent's @{tt}template_vars@{utt} set. A phase with a non-empty list allows only those
agents (and only those that @{tt}allowed_agents@{utt} allows); a phase not
in the map, or with an empty list, falls back to @{tt}_default@{utt}, and an empty or missing
@{tt}_default@{utt} allows every agent @{tt}allowed_agents@{utt} allows. No
phase set: no narrowing.

@{h2}Reloading

@{tt}agent-cli reload@{utt} (POST @{tt}/admin/reload-config@{utt}) applies
changed lists, limits, the timeout, the info settings, the hook settings
and phase filtering to a running SAM without touching its running
sub-agents. A new agent needs a restart.

@{h2}Where the data lives

A sub-agent's session is an ordinary session file of the caller's user in
the session store, linked to its parent. The parent's session keeps an
entry per sub-agent (type, times, state, task, current activity) under
@{tt}metadata.sub_agents@{utt}. Archiving keeps both.
@endnode

@node register "Making an agent spawnable"
@{h1}Making an agent spawnable

There is no global list: each SAM instance says which agents it may
start. For an agent to be started by another one through a SAM:

@{bullet}The agent is a server entry with @{tt}enabled: true@{utt} whose
type resolves -- otherwise "Agent type 'x' not found in registry". A new
entry needs a restart.
@{bullet}The SAM's @{tt}allowed_agents@{utt} names it (or a pattern
matches it) and @{tt}blocked_agents@{utt} does not; with phase filtering
the current phase allows it too. Otherwise @{tt}agent_blocked@{utt} or
@{tt}phase_blocked@{utt}. A reload is enough.
@{bullet}Its @{tt}metadata.visibility@{utt} is @{tt}tool@{utt},
@{tt}both@{utt} or @{tt}ui@{utt}: a private agent (the default) is left out of the
"Available" list, and the model never learns its name.
@{bullet}The calling agent's allowlist names the SAM: @{tt}"+<sam instance>/*"@{utt}.
@{bullet}If the agent sets @{tt}metadata.min_role@{utt}, the user of the
calling run has that role -- otherwise @{tt}agent_role_gate@{utt}, before a
session is made.

An example of a harness wiring its own roles:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    review_sam:
      type: sub_agent_manager
      enabled: true
      max_nesting_depth: 1
      max_sub_agents_per_type: 4
      default_wait_timeout: 1800
      allowed_agents:
        - code_reviewer

    code_reviewer:
      type: basic_agent
      enabled: true
      description: "Reviews a change and names the problems"
      metadata:
        visibility: tool

    lead:
      type: multi_turn_agent
      enabled: true
      agent_config:
        tools:
          allowed:
            - "+review_sam/*"
        hooks:
          enabled: true
          overrides:
            review_sam.inject_sub_agent_context:
              enabled: true
@{body}

A @{tt}max_nesting_depth@{utt} of 1 keeps the reviewers from starting
sub-agents of their own -- they own no SAM anyway, which is the surer
way.
@endnode
